World Maker

Type a prompt and get a small 3D world you can walk around in.

Features
Each world is a 10 x 10 meter floating island.
An agent places 3D models from your library in a single shot.
You walk in first person with WASD and jump with Space.
Every world is saved with its prompt, agent model, generation time, object count and a screenshot from the player's start view.
The home screen shows a gallery of past worlds. Click one to walk into it.

How to open it
You need Node 22, Claude Code installed and logged in, and an OpenAI API key.
Run npm install, then npx playwright install chromium.
Create a .env file with OPENAI_API_KEY and WORLD_LIBRARY_DIR (the folder with your 3D models).
Run npm run build-library once to index the models. Run it again after adding models.
Run npm start and open http://127.0.0.1:5173.

How it works
The server picks the library models closest to your prompt using OpenAI embeddings.
It launches the local claude CLI with those models and one MCP tool, build_world.
The agent calls build_world once with the ground, sky, spawn point and every object.
The server checks the layout, nudges objects back inside the island, drops ones that do not fit, and moves the spawn if something blocks it.
The browser gets the objects over server-sent events and renders them with three.js.
A headless Chromium renders the player's start view for the gallery thumbnail.
Worlds are saved in data/worlds.

Options
WORLD_AGENT_MODEL sets the agent model. The default is sonnet.
A model named river:<model> runs through River instead of Claude Code. It needs RIVER_API_KEY and a Python venv with river/requirements.txt.
node bench/run.mjs runs the same prompts through several models for comparison.
